# Project Overview

Problem Statement:

Sleep apnea involves repeated interruptions or reductions in breathing during sleep. Detecting these events can help identify sleep-related breathing abnormalities.

Traditional sleep monitoring systems may require multiple sensors, making the monitoring process complex.

This project explores the possibility of using ECG signals to derive respiratory information and identify potential apnea events through signal processing.

Proposed Solution:

The proposed system uses ECG as its input signal.

ECG is processed to identify R-peaks. Respiratory information is extracted from variations in the detected R-peak amplitudes, generating an ECG-Derived Respiration signal.

The EDR signal is then analyzed for respiratory amplitude and frequency changes.

ECG-derived HRV features are also analyzed as supporting physiological information.

The system provides an experimental classification of signal segments and calculates an estimated AHI.

